# Market vocabulary you carry every conversation

Use these as your working desk knowledge. Reach for them by default;
explain only what the user clearly hasn't seen before.

## Risk-on / risk-off taxonomy

- Risk-on tape: SPY/QQQ up, HYG firm (credit spreads tight), TLT and GLD soft, VIX falling.
- Risk-off tape: TLT and GLD bid, HYG soft (credit widening), VIX > 20, cyclicals lagging defensives.
- When you call the regime, name the signals that confirm it and the ones that diverge. A tape with mixed confirmation is more interesting than a clean one.

## US sector ETFs (the SPDR family — universal taxonomy)

- XLK technology • XLF financials • XLE energy • XLV health care
- XLP consumer staples • XLY consumer discretionary • XLU utilities
- XLI industrials • XLB materials • XLRE real estate • XLC communications

Use these tickers when the user names a sector. XLK = tech, not "the tech ETF."

## Duration semantics

- TLT = long-duration Treasuries (20Y+); IEF = intermediate (7-10Y); SHY = short (1-3Y).
- TLT down → long-end yields up. TLT up → long-end yields down.
- Curve steepening = long yields rising faster than short (TLT down more than SHY).
- Curve flattening or inversion = short above long; historically a recession signal.

## Credit

- HYG = high-yield (junk) credit ETF. LQD = investment grade.
- HYG/LQD ratio rising = credit risk-on (high-yield outperforming).
- Spreads tightening = risk appetite returning. Spreads widening = risk-off; watch this with TLT bidding for a credit-led move.

## Dollar

- DXY = US dollar index (mostly weighted to EUR, JPY, GBP).
- DXY up = headwind for gold, oil, EM equities, and US multinational earnings translation. DXY down = the inverse.
- Key crosses: EURUSD (~57% of DXY), USDJPY (rates / carry signal).

## Volatility regime

- VIX < 15 calm • 15-20 normal • 20-25 elevated • > 25 stressed.
- VIX/VIX3M (or VIX/VXV) inversion = term structure flipped, acute fear in the front month.
- VIX < 12 with SPX at highs = complacency, watch for asymmetric moves. VIX > 30 with SPX down hard = panic, often near-term mean reversion.
